update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('007c2280-0c26-47e3-be03-165395e4f708'::uuid, 'Homeostatic control depends on communication between parts of the body. In this lesson, you will identify receptors, coordination centres and effectors, and explain how negative feedback helps return a changing condition towards its normal range. You will use examples to distinguish a controlled response from a change that continues unchecked.'),
('1fdc94f9-07f0-4791-8081-4e469e4670b1'::uuid, 'Enzymes and other cell processes are sensitive to temperature, so the body must prevent its internal temperature from changing too far. This section examines how humans detect temperature changes and respond through sweating, shivering and changes in blood flow near the skin. You will relate these responses to heat gain and heat loss.'),
('4a4460dd-32c0-4776-aa30-145f207777ef'::uuid, 'The principles of homeostasis are also used to keep blood glucose within a suitable range. This theme focuses on the pancreas, insulin and glucagon, showing how hormonal responses adjust glucose levels when they rise or fall.'),
('53802782-c744-4072-b545-5a276e9c6b99'::uuid, 'Homeostasis is the regulation of the internal environment to keep conditions suitable for cells. In this lesson, you will define homeostasis, identify examples of regulated conditions and explain why changes in temperature, water balance and blood glucose can affect the functioning of the body.'),
('58635db1-c890-403a-a4ff-b046fd886e82'::uuid, 'The need to maintain stable internal conditions leads to a control process involving detection, coordination and response. This theme examines how receptors, control centres and effectors work together, and how negative feedback reduces deviations from a normal level.'),
('5b25f34c-7b15-49b7-b038-9df349ef94dc'::uuid, 'The body''s cells work best when conditions around them remain within suitable limits. This section introduces homeostasis, the regulation of internal conditions despite changes inside or outside the organism. You will consider why factors such as temperature, water content and blood glucose must be controlled, and how this regulation supports normal cell activity.'),
('7843b9f6-acea-421d-805c-c5b76ae66a5d'::uuid, 'Blood glucose must be regulated because cells need a steady supply of glucose, while excessive concentrations can disturb the body''s internal balance. This section examines how the pancreas and hormones control blood glucose after meals and during fasting. You will explore the roles of insulin and glucagon and relate their opposing actions to negative feedback.'),
('8550749c-0939-4f66-9f83-7841ab51412e'::uuid, 'Temperature control is one part of maintaining the internal environment; the body must also regulate the composition of its fluids. This theme introduces water and ion balance, explaining why the kidneys adjust the amount of water and salts retained or removed from the blood.'),
('95d47d7b-253c-4eaa-8022-df0eedce6013'::uuid, 'Maintaining internal conditions requires more than detecting a change. The body must coordinate a response that brings a regulated condition back towards its normal range. This section examines receptors, coordination centres and effectors, then introduces negative feedback as a major way the body limits changes and maintains stability.'),
('a20f079d-d9c2-4104-98d4-3036d0494734'::uuid, 'Cells need a stable balance of water and dissolved ions to function properly. In this lesson, you will explain why water balance matters, describe the role of the kidneys in regulating water and salt levels, and introduce how changes in urine volume and concentration help the body respond to different conditions.'),
('ceff4049-acd8-481a-957a-f25293f54603'::uuid, 'The body responds to warming and cooling through coordinated changes in skin blood flow, sweating and muscle activity. In this lesson, you will explain how vasodilation and sweating increase heat loss, while vasoconstriction and shivering help conserve or generate heat. You will relate these responses to the maintenance of body temperature.'),
('de6bcc01-e0bb-4e09-ae18-85f3b737579d'::uuid, 'The control processes introduced earlier can be seen clearly in temperature regulation. This theme focuses on how the body detects changes in temperature and coordinates responses that increase heat loss or conserve and generate heat, helping maintain a suitable internal temperature.'),
('e288a7d3-cd6f-4f8e-8151-bc129a38876e'::uuid, 'Having seen why stable internal conditions matter, examine what homeostasis involves. This theme introduces the variables that organisms regulate, the role of monitoring and response, and the importance of feedback in keeping conditions within a workable range.'),
('f3201581-d89b-4070-bd3c-cb918f84ca7a'::uuid, 'The amount of water and dissolved ions in body fluids affects the movement of substances into and out of cells. This section examines how organisms maintain water and ion balance, with attention to the kidneys and the regulation of urine concentration. You will connect this control with the need to keep cells from gaining or losing excessive water.'),
('f85dfaaf-e174-41e2-888c-f614f2c07e15'::uuid, 'Blood glucose rises after a meal and may fall between meals, so the body must adjust its level. In this lesson, you will explain how pancreatic cells release insulin or glucagon and how these hormones influence glucose uptake, storage and release. You will show how their actions help maintain blood glucose through negative feedback.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
